   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Consonant sounds",
     "texto": [
      "The «g» and the digraph «ch» are usually pronounced as rough velar or uvular fricatives (similar to the «ch» in Scottish «loch», though softer in the south and in Flanders). The group «sch» combines a clear «s» immediately followed by the rough «ch» sound, as in «school» (school) or «schip» (ship), never inserting a supporting vowel at the start. Note that it is not the English «sh» sound."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
